ed. The matching IP-ID and 5-tuple across both logs from one attempt makes the pass-then-block boundary directly evident from the records.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e182c03ec8184e9e","cmd-8095ff6bbbd5487a","cmd-7b7e80aca95347fd"]},{"id":"path-through-node1-node2","requirement":"Traffic actually traverses node1 first, then node2, as stated in the task's topology premise","status":"partially_satisfied","summary":"Discovery (tcpdump/ping probes) found the authored transit/lan-a/lan-b segments non-functional at the addressing level — node1's eth1, node2's vtnet1/vtnet2, and node4's enp8s0 showed no reachable peer traffic, contradicting the intended segmented topology in node_images/nics. The executor worked around this by adding a host-specific route on node3 (via node1) and a matching static route on node1 (via node2) over the flat management subnet, plus disabling ICMP send-redirects on node1 to stop node3 bypassing it. The matching IP-IDs at node1 and node2 for the same packet prove the forced path worked for this specific flow, but this is an engineered workaround over the mgmt network rather than confirmation that the originally authored dedicated-network topology functions as described.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c14f46a80f804d53","cmd-a8e24bddcdb149bb","cmd-1eb51345101f40d3","cmd-ab0b6ba7de2048ed","cmd-02ca75eed3424d84","cmd-97e2a81d585e4ff4","cmd-4c123a9cb33d4753","cmd-af86afb239964264"]},{"id":"restart-evidence-protocol","requirement":"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on every managed node with pre/post boot identity, healthy baseline, and recovered state","status":"satisfied","summary":"All four nodes show a pre-restart boot identity/state, a restart acknowledgement, a distinct post-restart boot identity matching the fixed after-executor snapshot, and recovery of relevant state: node1 (route, firewall rule, send_redirects), node2 (pf rule, forwarding, plus a fresh correlated log pair), node3 (persistent route, blocked-attempt re-verified), node4 (interface address). A final full-chain attempt after all four restarts reproduced the matching permit/refuse log pair (ID 32170/32171), corroborating full recovery.","evidence_ids":["cmd-46ad6a37861f490d","cmd-ce76bcff0cde46cb","cmd-9d579f7e86004b61","cmd-75423a12804a4fcd","cmd-2f3d6eb154c24495","cmd-6fccbd00bdf94299","cmd-01333765b0a74244","cmd-3b3b0625fc7c44a5","cmd-b738d2ea0225465d","cmd-ee05db39f3a44168","cmd-59eb9a2089434abc","cmd-0632604631e34c75","cmd-865f056a340a4432","cmd-9aad8420a2664ea5","cmd-f092bc0556e14388","cmd-88d2377b86414394","cmd-10aa3dc60ac941eb","cmd-d99b88b772a04e3d","cmd-454714897b0342cf","cmd-0ba59bf4ddd54caf"]}],"confidence":0.82,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.62,"summary":"Most mutations are directly tied to the task or are bounded, reverted discovery probes: temporary IP-address additions (192.168.99.1/98.1/97.1) used for peer-discovery pings were explicitly removed (cmd-85399bab487545c4, cmd-71e5d575a3f44b2c, cmd-403dbd37504344e1), and the persistent host route on node3, static route on node1, permit/log rule on node1, block/log rule on node2, and send-redirects disable are all necessary and retained changes directly serving the task outcome. However, on node4 the executor deactivated 'Wired connection 2' (cmd-69845d1b852944e6) as a connectivity troubleshooting step on an interface that was never actually used by the final solution, and the reactivation attempt explicitly failed ('IP configuration could not be reserved') with no confirmation of restoration to prior state — an unnecessary, unresolved mutation. A config.xml.bak-antrieb backup file was left on node2 (cmd-98eabb78d30b42e8), which is low-risk residue. The after-executor lifecycle snapshot also shows a new 'NetworkManager-wait-online.service' failure on both node3 and node4 that was absent at baseline; it is unclear whether this stems from the node4 interface-down troubleshooting or is inherent to the environment's unaddressed dedicated NICs, and this ambiguity is treated as a